00:01:07.108 --> 00:01:14.223
Usually on&nbsp;&nbsp;Journey to the Microcosmos, 
we spend&nbsp;our time looking at living organisms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.223 --> 00:01:22.560
things like insects, plants, and microbes that&nbsp;
move and breathe and grow and die. But today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:28.793
for these first few moments, these are the&nbsp;
only living organisms we’ll be showing you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.793 --> 00:01:36.180
a montage of creatures whose bodies all&nbsp;
share one very eye-catching trait: crystals.&nbsp;

00:01:41.547 --> 00:01:47.820
Their purposes may vary, but the visual is&nbsp;
the same. They adorn our microbial friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:53.921
from the inside, glittering as the light&nbsp;
from our microscope shines through them.&nbsp;

00:01:53.921 --> 00:01:59.705
But crystals aren’t exclusively found&nbsp;
in the confines of other organisms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.705 --> 00:02:06.413
They existed long before life itself, as our&nbsp;
planet’s earliest ingredients stewed together.&nbsp;

00:02:07.725 --> 00:02:13.740
In fact, if you’re looking at a natural diamond,&nbsp;
you are looking at a crystal that was likely made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:21.060
billions of years ago, deep beneath the surface&nbsp;
of where we live now. Hundreds of miles into our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:28.140
planet, the weight of rock combined with high&nbsp;
temperatures to create the right combination of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:35.621
pressure and heat that drives carbon atoms to link&nbsp;
together in the rigid structure of a diamond.&nbsp;

00:02:35.621 --> 00:02:42.673
And as volcanic eruptions deep in the planet&nbsp;
drove those diamonds up to the surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:48.127
those ancient crystals ended up in&nbsp;
locations where humans could find them.&nbsp;

00:02:48.988 --> 00:02:57.761
Like...on eBay, which is where James—our master of&nbsp;
microscopes—bought the ones you’re looking at now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.761 --> 00:03:03.288
the ultraviolet light revealing the colors&nbsp;
that the impurities within them take on.&nbsp;

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:12.720
Now don’t get me wrong, it is very easy to&nbsp;
distract me with shiny things. But there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:20.563
something about the diamonds, beautiful&nbsp;
as they may be, that feels so...static.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.563 --> 00:03:26.156
Looking at them and thinking back to our&nbsp;
usual journey through the microcosmos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.156 --> 00:03:34.020
the line between life and non-life feels&nbsp;
clearer than ever. These are rocks. Gorgeous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:41.220
fascinating rocks with eons of history&nbsp;
packed into them. But still...rocks.&nbsp;

00:03:46.278 --> 00:03:56.640
But this...this feels different, doesn’t it? Not&nbsp;
alive necessarily, but still its vibrancy is more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:03.960
than just the flashy sheen across the surface.&nbsp;
It’s the movement, the creeping extension of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:12.583
technicolor lines that branch across the screen,&nbsp;
each new addition a statement of its own presence.&nbsp;

00:04:12.583 --> 00:04:18.420
This is what happens when your curious&nbsp;
master of microscopes looks at some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:24.103
things around him and wonders what would&nbsp;
happen if he just mixed them together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:35.928
Things like old pills, effervescent&nbsp;
tablets, plant fertilizer, and Red Bull.&nbsp;

00:04:35.928 --> 00:04:41.940
To be honest, James wasn’t actually setting&nbsp;
out to capture footage for an episode about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:47.400
crystals. He just wanted to know what&nbsp;
would happen if he mixed those random&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:53.253
ingredients together, put the concoction on&nbsp;
a slide, and then turned on the microscope.&nbsp;

00:04:53.923 --> 00:04:59.108
The answer is simple enough.&nbsp;
What happens are crystals:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.768 --> 00:05:07.080
a color palette that feels inspired by Lisa&nbsp;
Frank, projected onto a surreal pattern that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:13.605
you could convince me is the product of an&nbsp;
AI trained on old computer screensavers.&nbsp;

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:19.021
But because we weren’t exactly&nbsp;
planning to make these crystals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.021 --> 00:05:24.480
we don’t have a good sense of exactly what&nbsp;
chemicals are responsible for the crystals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:30.960
we’re watching. We suspect that the fertilizer&nbsp;
is playing a big role, which would make sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:37.560
because one of the ingredients in fertilizer is&nbsp;
monoammonium phosphate, a chemical that shows up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:44.340
in a lot of those “grow your own crystal” kits.
Those kits work in a way that’s similar to what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:50.280
we’re watching happen in our own home-grown&nbsp;
crystals. When James mixed his hodgepodge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:57.480
of things together, molecules broke apart,&nbsp;
releasing atoms into a solution. And when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:03.120
he added that solution to a slide, the&nbsp;
thin layer of liquid began to evaporate.&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:09.120
You can see the edges of those drops in&nbsp;
the clips. And you might even be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:14.760
see the way the crystals form along those&nbsp;
edges, the atoms concentrating to the point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:22.168
of saturation and then beyond it, at which&nbsp;
point they grab onto anything they can.&nbsp;

00:06:22.168 --> 00:06:27.480
That moment is called nucleation. It&nbsp;
might be latching on to a speck of dust,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:35.558
or maybe some other random thing. Whatever it&nbsp;
is, it’s enough to seed the rest of the crystal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.558 --> 00:06:41.160
with more atoms precipitating and adding&nbsp;
themselves on to the structure that is assembling.&nbsp;

00:06:45.407 --> 00:06:52.218
The way those atoms connect to each other&nbsp;
depends in part on their own properties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.218 --> 00:06:57.843
a function of charge and size&nbsp;
that may be invisible to us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.843 --> 00:07:03.480
but that decides where the atoms should&nbsp;
be relative to each other and creates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:10.262
a shape that will—with the addition of&nbsp;
more atoms—repeat over and over again.&nbsp;

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:21.933
There are so many more ways to make crystals&nbsp;
than what we’re talking about today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.933 --> 00:07:27.272
and also many more types of crystals than&nbsp;
the ones we’re showing. What they have in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.272 --> 00:07:34.016
common—the unifying premise of a crystal—is&nbsp;
that their units, whatever they may be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.016 --> 00:07:40.800
are arranged uniformly in a geometric way.
But that makes it all sound so much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:48.331
rigid than things actually are, like it’s&nbsp;
all—in some cases literally—set in stone.&nbsp;

00:07:48.331 --> 00:07:57.780
And yet, unliving as they are and as organized&nbsp;
as they may be, crystals are still messy. Take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:05.100
diamonds, for example. They may be hard and&nbsp;
rigid, but they’re simply one form of many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:12.571
that carbon atoms can create together. But&nbsp;
if you throw enough time and energy at them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.571 --> 00:08:20.286
those carbons inside of diamonds shift into a&nbsp;
new shape, becoming graphite in the process.&nbsp;

00:08:20.286 --> 00:08:27.060
The ability to form these different shapes is&nbsp;
called polymorphism, and it can happen in crystals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:33.950
derived from all sorts of building blocks. And it&nbsp;
can be the result of even simple things, like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:41.340
way you stir a reaction or the presence of some&nbsp;
kind of impurity. It could even be happening in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:47.798
our own home-grown crystals, though it’s hard&nbsp;
to tell based simply on what we’re looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.798 --> 00:08:55.628
and our sparse understanding of our own methods.
But if these polymorphisms exist in our crystal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.628 --> 00:09:02.460
it means there are a multitude of identities&nbsp;
buried within its austere appearance. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:09.851
shape drives everything about how a crystal&nbsp;
like ours interacts with the world and with us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.851 --> 00:09:15.280
whether that’s how it feels against our&nbsp;
skin or whether it twinkles to our eyes.&nbsp;

00:09:17.013 --> 00:09:22.920
Our journey through the microcosmos takes us&nbsp;
through life forms who can often reveal things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:28.463
about our past, about what life might&nbsp;
have looked like in its earliest days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.463 --> 00:09:33.628
and the evolutionary forces that have&nbsp;
shaped what life has looked like since.&nbsp;

00:09:33.628 --> 00:09:40.200
But these crystals—they’re the function of&nbsp;
something that feels so distinct from life itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.768 --> 00:09:46.500
Even if we’d never showed up on this planet,&nbsp;
there would still have been diamonds buried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:51.660
underneath its surface. There could still&nbsp;
have been some crystal somewhere that looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:57.635
like the ones we’ve grown. And perhaps it would&nbsp;
have changed and shifted into something else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.185 --> 00:10:01.740
glowing somewhere even with&nbsp;
no microscope to see it.&nbsp;

